{
  "analysis": "REST with explicit verbose endpoints (A) offers strong cacheability via HTTP semantics, simpler tooling (standard HTTP caching, monitoring, rate-limiting per endpoint), clearer versioning boundaries, and lower implementation complexity on the server. It tends to produce over-fetching or under-fetching for complex client needs, and can require many round trips or bespoke endpoints for varied client views, increasing endpoint sprawl over time. GraphQL (B) offers a single flexible endpoint that lets clients request exactly the fields they need, reducing over/under-fetching and enabling rapid client-side iteration without new server endpoints. Trade-offs include more complex server-side query cost analysis (to prevent expensive/nested queries), harder HTTP-level caching (typically requires persisted queries or custom caching layers), a steeper learning curve for schema design and resolver implementation, and more nuanced authorization logic per field/type. Choice generally depends on factors such as: number and diversity of client applications (mobile, web, third-party), need for fine-grained data shaping, team familiarity with GraphQL tooling, caching/performance requirements, and API surface stability. If there are many heterogeneous clients with differing data needs and the team has capacity to manage query complexity/security, GraphQL provides more flexibility. If the API is simpler, cache-heavy, has few client types, or the team values operational simplicity and mature tooling, REST is often more practical.",
  "recommendation": "Depends on context: choose B (GraphQL) if multiple diverse clients need flexible data-fetching and the team can manage added complexity; choose A (REST) if the service has simpler, more uniform client needs and caching/operational simplicity is a priority. Without more detail on client diversity, team expertise, and performance constraints, a definitive single recommendation cannot be made objectively.",
